    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_7677e1136ef154a7ba244f2b71b8d501e664a7c9bcd72e7efce029471afe5e33",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Theorem 6 measures far-left zeta-prime exceptions with a weighted tail: a zero contributes according to how far it lies beyond a moving cutoff, so remote exceptions cost more than near-cutoff ones. It also gives a comparison on selected nearby endpoints, not uniformly on every interval. Choosing a cutoff that approaches the critical line on a logarithmic scale converts aggregate tail control into the stated conclusion that most complex zeta-prime zeros lie to its right. This gains prevalence information at a shrinking scale but neither excludes exceptional zeros nor determines their individual positions; the exact thresholds, exponents, and normalization are OCR-unsafe and remain to be scan-verified.",
      "parent_sources": [
